# ThreadSanitizer suppressions.
#
# big_atomic is a seqlock: sc() bumps the version to odd, writes val, then
# stores the version again, and ll()/load() read val speculatively and re-check
# the version afterwards.  That read and that write are deliberately not atomic
# -- being able to copy the value without a lock is the whole point -- so
# ThreadSanitizer reports them as a data race on every update.
#
# It could not validate the sequence even if the accesses were annotated:
# big_atomic orders them with standalone std::atomic_thread_fence, which
# ThreadSanitizer does not model.  GCC says so directly when building with
# -fsanitize=thread:
#
#   warning: 'atomic_thread_fence' is not supported with '-fsanitize=thread'
#
# So the suppression is not hiding a finding to be fixed later; it marks the
# part of the library that this tool cannot speak about.  Everything above
# big_atomic -- the epoch collector, the resizing protocol, the bucket logic --
# is still checked.
race:parlay::big_atomic
